# THE KERNEL.  Named $ROOT/unix and not by its config, because that is where
# the boot ROM looks and what mkimage used to copy from the builder.
#
# ON A MACHINE THAT IS ALREADY RUNNING ONE, THIS IS SKIPPED, and that is the
# point: ./installed is `test -f "$1"', so `mk world' with ROOT empty finds
# /unix present and never overwrites the kernel out from under the machine
# executing the recipe.  With ROOT=/v10 the disk is new and has none, so it is
# built.
#
# ipnx-v10.m NEEDS THE PATCHED TAB.  It declares `pt 64' and lib/tab:34 leaves
# `# cdev 18 pt' commented, and readconf.c:73 makes a name absent from the tab a
# hard error -- not a warning -- so mkconf would refuse the config outright.
# ./patch adds that one line, derived from the tape's own file.
# ONE CHAIN, AND THE .u IS NOT KEPT.  v10's ld writes its output even with
# symbols undefined -- it reports them and clears the execute bits -- so an
# `|| echo' on the link and a cp on the next line installs a kernel that did
# not link, and ./installed being `test -f' then calls /v10/unix done and
# never reaches $KERN again.  That is exactly what a missing _spcdev did
# here.  mk's exit status is ld's verdict, so && carries it; and the .u is
# removed either way, because a broken one left in astro/ is newer than its
# prerequisites and the next run would call THAT up to date too.
$ROOT/unix:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP $KERN
	cd $SYS/astro; mk -f ../lib/mk.star ipnx-v10.u && cp ipnx-v10.u $ROOT/unix || echo 'unix: FAILED'
	cd $SYS/astro; rm -f ipnx-v10.u || :

# ============================================================================
# THE REST OF THE DISTRIBUTION
#
# Everything above is what a v10 disk needs to boot, shut down cleanly and
# rebuild itself.  Everything below is the rest of what the tapes carry: the
# libraries under /usr/src, and every entry in /usr/src/cmd that Admin/Mk
# knows how to build.
#
# ADMIN/Mk IS THE SPEC, and it is thirty lines long.  Its loop takes names on
# the command line, asks Admin/lookline whether the name is in Admin/large
# (CFLAGS=-O) or not (CFLAGS=-Od2), asks Admin/dest where it goes, and then
# dispatches on the suffix:
#
#	*.y	yacc $B.y && cc $CFLAGS -o $B y.tab.c -ly
#	*.l	lex  $B.l && cc $CFLAGS -o $B lex.yy.c -ll
#	*.c	           cc $CFLAGS -o $B $B.c
#	*.s	as -o $B.o $B.s && cc -o $B $B.o
#	*.sh	cp $B.sh $DESTDIR$D/$B
#	dir	cd $i; make clean && make && make install && make clean
#
# and install() is `strip $1 && cp $1 $2'.  Admin/dest ends `else echo
# /usr/bin', so anything in none of the four lists goes there.
#
# WHY THE RULES BELOW ARE NOT `sh Admin/Mk name'.  Admin/dest prints an
# ABSOLUTE path -- /bin, /etc, /lib, /usr/lib, /usr/bin -- and Mk's install()
# passes it to cp with no DESTDIR (only the *.sh branch has one).  Running Mk
# under ROOT=/v10 would therefore install into the BUILDER's own /bin.  The
# dispatch is reproduced here instead, one rule per target, so the destination
# is $ROOT's.
#
# A NAME THAT COLLIDES WITH AN ENTRY IN cmd/ IS BUILT UNDER A `bin' SUFFIX.
# Sixteen of the flat sources have an output name that already exists in
# /usr/src/cmd: ed and sort are DIRECTORIES, and byteyears, cpio, cron,
# dcheck, dis, dmesg, last, logtty, ls, pr, ranlib, setpart, test and wall are
# the norman tape's own prebuilt binaries.  `cc -o ls ls.c' in that directory
# either fails with `ld: ls: Is a directory' or silently overwrites a tape
# artefact, so each is compiled as lsbin, disbin and so on and copied from
# there -- which is what ed, sort and ranlib above already do.
#
# FIVE FLAT SOURCES ARE ANSI C AND ARE COMPILED WITH lcc, not cc.  checkeq.c,
# idiff.c, strings.c, tail.c and zero.c all define main with a prototype --
# `main(int argc, char **argv)' -- and ccom/common/cgram.y:377-381 has
# `name_list: NAME | name_list CM NAME', an identifier list with no types in
# it at all; line 360 is `uerror("arg list in declaration")'.  So this
# compiler cannot parse them.  idiff.c and zero.c also want <stdlib.h>, which
# only /usr/include/lcc has and which lcc reaches by passing -I itself.
#
# NOT BUILT, and each for a header the corpus does not contain anywhere:
#   charge.c    <sys/share.h> and <sys/charges.h>, the Share Scheduler's own
#               headers -- charge.1 is `.TH CHARGE 1 SHARE'.  Neither is in
#               /usr/include, in /usr/sys/sys (which has a hundred others), or
#               anywhere in either tape tree.  THE KERNEL CANNOT COMPILE
#               EITHER: lsys/os/share.C:5-8 includes sys/param.h, sys/lnode.h,
#               sys/share.h and sys/charges.h, and lsys/os/limits.C:10-12 the
#               same, so the gap is in the tapes and not in cmd/.  sys/lnode.h
#               is the third of that set and the one ./patch already
#               reconstructs, from lnode(5) and Bell Labs' object code; the
#               other two would each need the same treatment.  charge.c also
#               wants <trace.h>, which exists only as cmd/trace/trace.h and
#               which cmd/trace's install does not copy anywhere
#   dumpcatch.c <sys/dumpl.h>, and there is nothing to reconstruct it from.
#               dumpcatch.c:5 says "the dump buffers in the dumpld line
#               discipline (see /usr/sys/dev/dumpld.c)" and no file called
#               dumpld exists in v10/ or in either tape tree -- no driver, no
#               header.  It reads the kernel by nlist for _dumpld and
#               _ndumpbuf, which this kernel does not export either
#   iostat.c    <sys/ubavar.h>, <sys/mbavar.h>; it also reads ubdinit, mbdinit
#               and the dk_* counters, which no v10 config has
#   agh.c       NOT ON THE MACHINE.  Both it and ofsck.c are norman's alone --
#   ofsck.c     neither is in secombe/cmd -- and mktape takes only thirteen
#               named directories from v10src (`tar xf $TAPES/v10src.tar
#               cmd/bison cmd/cp ... cmd/adb/vax'), not its loose files, so
#               /usr/src/cmd has neither.  Their rules answered `0: No source
#               file agh.c'.  Both were in the list because it was drawn from
#               the repository's merged tree rather than from what the build
#               actually sees
# and wc.s, because wc.c is the same program eight days newer -- Aug 26 1987
# against Aug 18 1987 -- and both would install /usr/bin/wc.
# ============================================================================

# ---- the libraries ---------------------------------------------------------
# Admin/ulibfiles is /usr/lib's own list; the trees that build its archives sit
# beside libc under /usr/src, not under cmd, so Admin/Mk never reaches them --
# its loop runs `cd /usr/src/cmd' first.  Each rule below is that tree's own
# build file with the install step done here, because almost every one of them
# writes to an absolute /usr/lib and would fill the BUILDER's.
#
# THE MATH LIBRARY IS A STUB, AND THAT IS THE TAPE'S OWN DOING.  libm/makefile
# opens "libm is now a dummy for those who still want to say -lm", and libc's
# mkfile:10-56 carries asin.o atan.o exp.o log.o pow.o sin.o sinh.o sqrt.o
# besjn.o and the rest: the math moved into libc.  So -lm resolves and adds
# nothing, which is the point of shipping it.
#
# NOT BUILT:
#   asm.libm  the older assembler math library.  It builds a libm.a of its own
#             from the same names and installs it to the same path, so it would
#             put the superseded copy over the stub.
#   libnm     ASM.LIBM'S TWIN, and it should never have had a rule here.
#             libnm.a IS IN NO ADMIN LIST -- Admin/ulibfiles does not name it,
#             so nothing on the tape asks for it in /usr/lib -- and its README
#             is "20 Aug 80, Robert Henry, UCB", the same 1980 library.  Ten of
#             its twelve names are libc members already: libc/math has asin,
#             atan, exp, log, pow, sin, sinh, sqrt, tan and tanh, and libc's
#             mkfile:10-56 lists their objects.  Only acos and cbrt are its
#             own.
#
#             IT ALSO HANGS THE BUILD.  `as -o sinh.o sinh.s' does not return:
#             a file containing nothing but
#                     .data
#                     .double 0d0.99999999999999998116e+0
#             reproduces it.  asscan.c:823-828 collects at most 63 characters
#             into fltchr and then calls atof, so the loop is not in the
#             assembler -- atof is /usr/src/build/src/libc/gen/atof.c, four
#             lines whose body is `return strtod(s, (char **)0)', and that
#             strtod is David M. Gay's, replaced by this project along with
#             _dtoa.  fconv.h picks the right arithmetic (`#ifdef vax' ->
#             VAX, P 56, Bias 129) and CC/float.h the right format (DBL_MANT_DIG
#             56, DBL_MAX_EXP 127, FLT_ROUNDS 1), so the fault is inside the
#             correction loop, not in its configuration.  Nothing else in the
#             build reaches it: libnm and asm.libm are the only sources with a
#             long-mantissa 0d constant, and every other one -- cmd/pico and
#             cmd/qsnap's fsqrt.s, libc/stdio/s.s and ostdio/doprnt.s -- is
#             0d4, 0d0.5 or 0d1.0.
#   ncurses   System V curses and terminfo, a different package.  Its top level
#             has no makefile, Makefile or mkfile at all -- only libcurses.mk --
#             so `make' there finds nothing, and its libcurses.a would land on
#             the Berkeley one Admin/ulibfiles names.
#   libplot/oldplot   no build file.
ULIB=$ROOT/usr/lib/liby.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libl.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libln.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libnetb.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libostdio.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libm.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libmp.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libdbm.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libcbt.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libcurses.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermlib.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libsdb.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libcc.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libj.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libpicfile.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libF77.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libI77.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libplot.a $ROOT/usr/lib/lib4014.a $ROOT/usr/lib/lib2621.a $ROOT/usr/lib/lib5620.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libblit.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libpen.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libram.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libtr.a
ulib:V: $ULIB

# liby is yacc's runtime -- Mk's *.y branch links -ly -- and libl is lex's.
$ROOT/usr/lib/liby.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/liby; make clean || :; make liby.a && cp liby.a $ROOT/usr/lib/liby.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/liby.a || echo 'liby.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/liby; make clean || :

# libnetb.a IS UNDER netfs/, NOT cmd/, which is why Admin/Mk never reaches it
# and why nothing here built it before.  cmd/face/mkfile:8 is
#	$CC -o faced $LDFLAGS faced.o -lnetb -lipc
# and it is the only -lnetb on the tapes outside netfs' own serv/.  The
# makefile's install copies rf.h to /usr/include as well as the archive to
# /usr/lib, and takes no DESTDIR, so both are done here against $ROOT.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libnetb.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/netfs/libnetb; make clean || :; make libnetb.a || echo 'libnetb.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/netfs/libnetb; cp rf.h $ROOT/usr/include/rf.h || :
	cd $SRC/netfs/libnetb; cp libnetb.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libnetb.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libnetb.a || :
	cd $SRC/netfs/libnetb; make clean || :

# libostdio.a IS THE OLD printf FAMILY -- atof, doprnt, doscan, fprintf,
# printf, scanf, sprintf -- and nothing on the tapes links -lostdio; it is
# built because its own mkfile installs it, `install:V: $DESTDIR/lib/
# libostdio.a' over DESTDIR=/usr.  Its doprnt rule reads doprnt.S, which the
# extractor renamed: doprnt.S and doprnt.s collided, the lowercase spelling
# kept the name and the other became u_doprnt.S.  ./patch points the rule at
# it.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libostdio.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $LIBC/stdio/ostdio; mk install DESTDIR=$ROOT/usr || echo 'libostdio.a: FAILED'
	cd $LIBC/stdio/ostdio; mk clean || :

# libl.a's rule has no prerequisites and runs its own five cc lines.
#
# IT INSTALLS libln.a AS WELL, from the same build.  Two makefiles link -lln --
# cmd/pret/makefile:23 and cmd/struct/makefile:37, the latter for beautify --
# and v10's ld hardcodes -lX to /lib, /usr/lib, /usr/local/lib with no -L
# (ld.c:1579-1595), so /usr/lib/libln.a has to exist under that name.  The tape
# does ship one, /usr/src/libl/libln.a, but its Makefile neither builds nor
# installs it: `all: libl.a' and an install of libl.a alone.  That archive is a
# prebuilt, and it is the SAME library -- its five members are allprint.o
# main.o reject.o yyless.o yywrap.o, in that order, which is exactly the five
# objects and the order of the ar line above it, and they define _allprint
# _printable _sprint, _main, _yyreject _yyracc, _yyless and _yywrap and nothing
# else.  So the name is installed from source rather than by copying a 1986
# binary onto the disk.  ranlib runs at the destination, not before the copy.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libl.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libl; make libl.a && cp libl.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libl.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libl.a || echo 'libl.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libl; cp libl.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libln.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libln.a || echo 'libln.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libl; rm -f libl.a || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/libm.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libm; make clean || :; make libm.a && cp libm.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libm.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libm.a || echo 'libm.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libm; make clean || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/libmp.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libmp; make clean || :; make libmp.a && cp libmp.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libmp.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libmp.a || echo 'libmp.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libmp; make clean || :

# libdbm.a IS A BARE OBJECT, not an archive: its rule is `mv dbm.o libdbm.a'.
# ranlib would have nothing to index and says so, so it is not run.  dbm.h is
# already in the include tree the $INC rule installs.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libdbm.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libdbm; make clean || :; make libdbm.a && cp libdbm.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libdbm.a || echo 'libdbm.a: FAILED'

# libcbt builds through a .c.x suffix rule that ar's each member as it goes,
# so `all' is the target and there is no separate link step.  cbt.h is already
# in the include tree.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libcbt.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libcbt; make clean || :; make all && cp libcbt.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libcbt.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libcbt.a || echo 'libcbt.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libcbt; rm -f *.x *.o || :

# THE ARCHIVE IS CALLED crlib.  libcurses/Makefile:49 is `crlib: ${OBJS}' and
# its install is `cp crlib ${DESTDIR}/usr/lib/libcurses.a' -- the name gets its
# .a only at the destination.  A .a is not evidence of a link library on these
# tapes and the reverse holds too.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libcurses.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libcurses; make clean || :; make crlib && cp crlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libcurses.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libcurses.a || echo 'libcurses.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libcurses; make clean || :

# Likewise termcap.a, which installs under two names -- libtermcap.a and a hard
# link libtermlib.a -- because both spellings are in Admin/ulibfiles and
# programs on these tapes use both (-ltermcap and -ltermlib).
$ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libtermlib; make clean || :; make termcap.a && cp termcap.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a || echo 'libtermcap.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libtermlib; make clean || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/libtermlib.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a
	rm -f $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermlib.a || :
	ln $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermcap.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libtermlib.a || echo 'libtermlib.a: FAILED'

# libsdb.a IS ONE ASSEMBLER FILE: `as dbxxx.s -o libsdb.a'.  Not an archive,
# so no ranlib.  cc.c:281-282 links it for -g, which is why a -g build with no
# libsdb.a reports `ld:/usr/local/lib/libsdb.a: cannot open'.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libsdb.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libsdb; make clean || :; make libsdb.a && cp libsdb.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libsdb.a || echo 'libsdb.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libsdb; make clean || :

# libcc's Makefile looks like an mkfile and is not one: `%.t: %.c' with `$%.c'
# for the stem and `$(ARCHIVE):& $(OBJ)' are all v10 MAKE -- misc.c:77,474,523
# handle %, and gram.y:5,121 make & a separator.  It has no clean target and
# the tape ships the .t stamps already touched, so `ar r libcc.a *.o' would run
# with no objects and the glob would reach ar unexpanded; the stamps are
# removed first.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libcc.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libcc; rm -f *.t *.o libcc.a install ship || :
	cd $SRC/libcc; make libcc.a && cp libcc.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libcc.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libcc.a || echo 'libcc.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libcc; rm -f *.o || :

# libj is the 5620's host-side library; its install also puts jerq.h in
# /usr/include, which the include tree the $INC rule copies already carries.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libj.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libj; make clean || :; make libj.a && cp libj.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libj.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libj.a || echo 'libj.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libj; make clean || :

# libpicfile is an mkfile, and an aggregate one: every member is $L(x.o) and
# the rule ars each as it compiles.  Its CFLAGS is -g, which is harmless here
# because nothing is linked -- cc.c:281-282 only adds -lsdb when it links.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libpicfile.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libpicfile; rm -f libpicfile.a || :
	cd $SRC/libpicfile; mk libpicfile.a && cp libpicfile.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libpicfile.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libpicfile.a || echo 'libpicfile.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libpicfile; rm -f *.o || :

# libF77 and libI77 are f2c's Fortran runtime.  libF77's mkfile sets CC=lcc
# itself (mkfile:3) and runs `ld -r -x' over each object; libI77's sets CC=cc.
# Both are left as they are.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libF77.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP $LCCOK
	cd $SRC/libF77; rm -f libF77.a *.o || :
	cd $SRC/libF77; mk libF77.a && cp libF77.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libF77.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libF77.a || echo 'libF77.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libF77; rm -f *.o || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/libI77.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libI77; rm -f libI77.a *.o || :
	cd $SRC/libI77; mk libI77.a && cp libI77.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libI77.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libI77.a || echo 'libI77.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libI77; rm -f *.o || :

# EIGHT PLOT LIBRARIES, ONE PER DEVICE, each built by its own makefile.  They
# all have the same shape --
#	lib4014.a: tek.c.a
#		mkdir xplot
#		cd xplot; ar x ../tek.c.a
#		cd xplot; cc -c -O *.c
#		cd xplot; ar rc ../lib4014.a *.o
#		rm -r xplot
# -- and it works because each .c.a carries its own header as a member (tek.h,
# hp.h, jcom.h, jplot.h, pen.h, ram.h, tr.h), so `ar x' puts the sources and
# the header they include in the same scratch directory.  v10's cpp cannot
# resolve a quoted include from outside the source's own directory, which is
# why they are packaged that way.
#
# THE .c.a ARE ARCHIVES ON THE MACHINE, WHATEVER THEY LOOK LIKE IN THE
# REPOSITORY.  v10-tree.py unpacks every ar member as a file, so in the
# checked-in tree plot.c.a is a DIRECTORY with an ORDER file beside the
# members; but mktape fills /usr/src straight from the six tapes
# (`cd /v10/usr/src && tar xf $TAPES/secombe.tar'), so what the build sees is
# the archive the tape holds.  Rules written for the unpacked form fail here
# with `sh: /usr/src/libplot/libplot/plot.c.a: bad directory'.
#
# The scratch directory is removed first: `mkdir xplot' is the first line of
# each recipe and fails if a previous run left one, and only lib4014, lib5620,
# libblit, libpen and libtr clear it in their own clean.  libplot extracts in
# place instead and has no xplot at all.
#
# The installs are done here because four of these makefiles write an absolute
# /usr/lib and two of them (lib4014, libtr) do it with mv, which would take the
# archive away from the tree that just built it.
$ROOT/usr/lib/libplot.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libplot/libplot; make clean || :
	cd $SRC/libplot/libplot; make libplot.a && cp libplot.a $ROOT/usr/lib/libplot.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/libplot.a || echo 'libplot.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libplot/libplot; make clean || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/lib4014.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib4014; rm -rf xplot || :; make clean || :
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib4014; make lib4014.a && cp lib4014.a $ROOT/usr/lib/lib4014.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/lib4014.a || echo 'lib4014.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib4014; rm -rf xplot || :; make clean || :

$ROOT/usr/lib/lib2621.a:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib2621; rm -rf xplot || :; make clean || :
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib2621; make lib2621.a && cp lib2621.a $ROOT/usr/lib/lib2621.a && ranlib $ROOT/usr/lib/lib2621.a || echo 'lib2621.a: FAILED'
	cd $SRC/libplot/lib2621; rm -rf xplot || :; make clean || :

# lib5620 and libblit are the same blit.c.a under two names -- the 5620 is the
