# ---- the directories in /usr/src/cmd ---------------------------------------
# Mk's last branch is
#	*)	if [ ! -d $i ] ...
#		else	cd $i; /bin/make clean && make && make install && make clean
# -- MAKE, not mk, and main.c:219-220 reads `makefile' then `Makefile', so a
# directory carrying only an mkfile is one the 1985 script cannot build at all.
# Those are driven with mk here, which is what the tape's own later trees
# expect (cmd/cpp, cmd/diff, cmd/sh and cmd/ps above already do it).
#
# `make install' IS NOT USED except where the makefile takes a variable for its
# destination.  Almost every one of these installs writes an absolute path --
# `cp a.out /usr/bin/eqn' -- so under ROOT=/v10 it would fill the BUILDER's
# /usr/bin and leave the new disk empty.  Where the makefile parameterises the
# path (DESTDIR, D, BIN, LIB, ROOT, DEST) that variable is set on the command
# line and its own install target runs; otherwise the product is built by name
# and copied here.  Which of the two applies is recorded per rule only where it
# is not obvious from the recipe.
#
# NOT BUILT, with the reason:
#   Admin        Mk's own case for it is `echo Do nothing.'
#   adb ccom lcc mk   built above, from the subdirectory that has the build file
#   as c2 cp cpp diff ed egrep expr gcc make ps sed sh strip termcap yacc
#                built above, each with the evidence for its own deviation
#   sort         the POSIX release, and its Makefile has no install target at
#                all.  /usr/bin/sort is built from cmd/sort.c, which is the
#                name Admin/large:23 carries
#   osh ops      the previous sh and ps.  osh's install is `mv /bin/sh
#                /bin/osh; cp sh /bin/sh' and ops' is `cp ps /bin' -- both
#                replace a command this disk has already installed
#   error        its install target is `echo "I won't do it"', with the two
#                real lines commented out.  The tape declines; so do we
#   chuck        `if test `cat /etc/whoami` != bowell; then echo not bowell;
#                exit 1; fi' -- it will only install on one machine in Murray
#                Hill, and it is not this one
#   view2d       MCC=/usr/jerq/bin/3cc, the MAC-32 cross compiler for the
#                5620.  It is not missing -- usr/jerq/sgs/3cc.c is its source
#                and jerq/sgs/makefile:10-12 installs it as /usr/jerq/bin/3cc
#                -- but jerq/ is not under /usr/src/cmd, so Admin/Mk's loop
#                never reaches it and nothing here builds it.  DEFERRED until
#                the jerq tree is built; /usr/jerq/bin does not exist yet
#   server       Datakit.  makefile:5 is `$(CC) -o server server.o -ldk',
#                server.c:1 is `#include <dk.h>', :39 calls tdkexec and :41-42
#                read dkerror -- all of them libdk's -- and its own comment at
#                :6 says what it is for: "on another cpu on Datakit".  This
#                machine's network is the Interlan, and cmd/track.c above is
#                the 1987 port off Datakit onto libipc precisely because of it
#   dump         Berkeley's fstab, which v10 does not have.  dumpoptr.c:291
#                is
#                    if (   (strcmp(fsp->fs_type, FSTAB_RW) == 0)
#                        || (strcmp(fsp->fs_type, FSTAB_RO) == 0) ){
#                and /usr/include/fstab.h:16-22 gives struct fstab as fs_spec,
#                fs_file, fs_ftype, fs_flags, fs_passno -- an INT fs_ftype,
#                with FSNONE (-1) and FSSWAP (-2) for its special values.  BSD
#                keeps a `char *fs_type' string and compares it against
#                FSTAB_RW and FSTAB_RO; neither the member nor either constant
#                exists anywhere in v10, which is the three errors the compiler
#                gives on that one line.  Two different fstab formats
#   PDP11        A cross toolchain for a machine this one does not emulate,
#                and only half of it is here.  README:1-2 is "PDP11 utilities
#                package for the VAX (Non-portable Parts) / Release 3  13 may
#                1981  Steve Hartwell", and its Step 1 says "you need two
#                tapes: this one, and the tape which has the pdp11 c compiler,
#                assembler, archiver, and various portable utilities" --
#                Makefile's init target carries the same note, `# Assumes that
#                c compiler tape already installed', and renames that tape's
#                files with `mv c 11c; mv as 11as'.  Even with 11as/ and 11c/
#                present, Makefile:48-49 is `11crt0.o: 11crt0.s' / `11as -o
#                11crt0.o 11crt0.s', calling 11as through PATH, while the 11as
#                target only does `cd 11as; make all' and leaves the binary in
#                the subdirectory: `all' cannot reach 11crt0.o without an
#                install having gone first, which is `Make: Cannot load 11as'
#   ap           the AP newswire.  apkeys is the wire-feed side, taking the
#                Associated Press circuit off a serial line and filing stories
#                into /usr/spool/ap; apnews is the reader over that spool.
#                Nothing here has the wire.  cmd/apsend is a different
#                directory and is still built
#   dag vsw      C++, and no cfront to translate it.  The bootstrap AT&T
#                shipped -- cfront's own output for its own source, which a
#                native cc compiles into a translator -- is on none of the six
#                tapes, and Cpre before it is not here either.  Both were being
#                put through lcc so the build would say what it hit; the answer
#                is `//' comments, which lcc/c/lex.c:297-315 lexes as two
#                divisions, and X::f members in vsw's crossbar.c, dynasty.c,
#                line.c and switch.c and dag's edge_t.c, node_t.c and util.c,
#                with a class in dag_start.c
#   weather      alice's, and a research service rather than a command:
#                weather(7) "reports current weather conditions and a forecast
#                for towns in the contiguous United States" from an index this
#                machine has no feed for.  cmd/weather.sh, the remote wrapper,
#                is one line -- `exec alice weather "$*"'
#   pico         Makefile:12 links -lfb, and libfb is on no tape: the header
#                is there as /usr/include/fb.h, the implementation is not.
#                Holzmann's own release of the VAX pico -- spinroot.com/pico/
#                pico.tar.gz, cm.bell-labs.com being long dead -- is byte for
#                byte this same tree and carries no libfb either.  Its Readme
#                names the hardware, "a VAX-750 connected to a metheus
#                framebuffer", and calls framebuffers/ "some support for other
#                types of framebuffers as well, but really quite minimal": that
#                subdirectory is Ramtek and friends, not the library.  The pico
#                binary shipped beside the source settles it -- its symbol table
#                holds _inset _raddp _rsubp _rcanon _rdiv _rmul _rdivp _rmulp,
#                fb.h:115's declaration list exactly, with _closepic _ptinrect
#                _rectXrect _rectclip, and not one symbol from framebuffers/.
#                popi, the portable rewrite from Holzmann's 1988 book, shares no
#                file with this pico and has no libfb of its own
#   poly         Makefile makes polypr and polypic depend on /usr/include/
#                poly.h, and polypr.c:3 and polypic.c:3 are `#include <poly.h>'.
#                No poly.h exists on any tape or anywhere in the superset
#   post.src  cmd/postscript above.  The same DWB PostScript package a year
#             NEWER -- VERSION 3.3.2, Jul 7 1992, against 3.3.1, May 21 1991 --
#             and it installs the same paths, so whichever ran second would
#             land on the other
#   docsubmit hdr lost+found netnews nupas sml spin udemon upas uudiff
#                no makefile, Makefile or mkfile AT THE TOP LEVEL, which is the
#                only level Mk's `cd $i; /bin/make' reads.  ap, basic, pcc1,
#                postscript, cref, lfactor and weather were in this list too
#                and are built below, each from where its build file or its
#                own source says.  Of the ten left: netnews is B-news 2.10 and
#                nupas and upas are the mail system, fifteen machine-specific
#                subdirectories apiece with a uucp/ in each -- mail, news and
#                uucp are not carried on this disk.  hdr is not a program at
#                all but cyntax's header set (defs.h opens "Sydney C
#                Compiler").  sml is Standard ML of New Jersey 0.44, shipped
#                with prebuilt mo.m68, mo.ns32, mo.sparc and mo.vax tarballs.
#                spin is one README: the source "is on pyxis in /netlib/spin",
#                which "should always be taken as the master copy".
